WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.230
Inside every neural network, between one layer

00:00:03.091 --> 00:00:05.127
and the next, sits a matrix.

00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:08.339
A vector of activations goes in, the matrix moves

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.156
it, and a new vector comes out.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.456
GPT-two is a stack of hundreds of them; the model

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:14.240
reading a C.T.

00:00:14.101 --> 00:00:15.808
scan is a stack of them too.

00:00:15.995 --> 00:00:18.157
So what does a matrix actually do?

00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:19.861
Not a table of numbers.

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:22.543
A machine that moves all of space at once.

00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:25.407
Feed it the whole plane, and the whole plane moves,

00:00:25.461 --> 00:00:28.100
with grid lines staying straight and evenly spaced.

00:00:28.292 --> 00:00:30.158
And the machine is easy to read.

00:00:30.372 --> 00:00:33.624
Its first column is where the arrow one, zero lands.

00:00:33.837 --> 00:00:36.658
Its second column is where zero, one lands.

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:39.612
Know those two, and you know where everything goes.

00:00:41.049 --> 00:00:44.733
Take the matrix with rows two, one, and zero, one.

00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:48.055
Its columns are two, zero, and one, one.

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:50.475
Apply it to the vector one, two.

00:00:50.689 --> 00:00:53.702
The rule says: one times the first column, plus

00:00:53.564 --> 00:00:55.044
two times the second column.

00:00:55.208 --> 00:00:58.369
One times two, zero, is two, zero.

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:01.449
Two times one, one, is two, two.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.551
Add them: four, two.

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:05.548
That is where the vector lands.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.843
Watch the grid move with it: the x direction stretches

00:01:08.705 --> 00:01:11.230
to twice its length, and the y direction leans

00:01:11.091 --> 00:01:11.332
to the right.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:14.010
The one property that makes all of this work is

00:01:13.872 --> 00:01:14.363
linearity.

00:01:14.224 --> 00:01:17.419
The matrix of a sum is the sum of the matrices.

00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:20.056
Scale the input, and the output scales.

00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:23.374
That is why the grid lines stay straight, and why

00:01:23.474 --> 00:01:24.658
the two columns are enough.

00:01:26.682 --> 00:01:28.434
Now do two moves in a row.

00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:30.376
First stretch x by two.

00:01:30.586 --> 00:01:32.589
Then rotate by ninety degrees.

00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:35.282
There is a single matrix that does both at once,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.384
and it is the product: the rotation matrix, times

00:01:38.245 --> 00:01:39.430
the stretch matrix.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.964
Read right to left, like functions.

00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:43.468
Multiply them out.

00:01:43.682 --> 00:01:47.309
The rotation is zero, minus one, one, zero.

00:01:47.492 --> 00:01:50.539
The stretch is two, zero, zero, one.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.753
Row times column: zero, minus one; two, zero.

00:01:54.966 --> 00:01:57.764
That single matrix sends the grid exactly where

00:01:57.625 --> 00:01:58.786
the two moves did.

00:01:58.958 --> 00:02:01.677
Swap the order, rotate first and then stretch,

00:02:01.765 --> 00:02:04.801
and you get a different matrix: zero, minus two;

00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:06.006
one, zero.

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:07.722
The grids do not match.

00:02:07.935 --> 00:02:10.801
Matrix multiplication does not commute, because

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.870
doing things in a different order is a different

00:02:12.731 --> 00:02:13.256
thing.

00:02:13.469 --> 00:02:16.381
That is why a network's layers cannot be reordered.

00:02:17.818 --> 00:02:20.036
How much does the machine stretch area?

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:21.725
Follow the unit square.

00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:24.668
Under our matrix it becomes a parallelogram of

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.430
area two.

00:02:25.643 --> 00:02:28.975
That number has a formula, a d minus b c, and a

00:02:28.836 --> 00:02:30.407
name: the determinant.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.941
Here, two times one, minus one times zero.

00:02:34.132 --> 00:02:34.691
Two.

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:37.616
If the determinant is zero, the square is squashed

00:02:37.477 --> 00:02:38.695
flat onto a line.

00:02:38.886 --> 00:02:39.843
Area gone.

00:02:40.057 --> 00:02:42.514
And once it is gone, nothing can bring it back:

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.457
a matrix with determinant zero has no inverse.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:48.718
For any other, the inverse is the machine run backwards,

00:02:48.795 --> 00:02:50.639
and it returns every point home.

00:02:52.057 --> 00:02:54.536
Most arrows get turned by a matrix.

00:02:54.723 --> 00:02:55.861
A few do not.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:58.543
They stay on their own line and only get longer

00:02:58.405 --> 00:02:59.225
or shorter.

00:02:59.416 --> 00:03:02.191
Those are the eigenvectors, and the stretch factor

00:03:02.052 --> 00:03:03.281
is the eigenvalue.

00:03:03.484 --> 00:03:05.907
A times v equals lambda times v.

00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:09.734
Take the symmetric matrix two, one, one, two.

00:03:09.913 --> 00:03:13.040
To find lambda, ask when A minus lambda I squashes

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:15.449
space: its determinant must be zero.

00:03:15.627 --> 00:03:19.607
Two minus lambda, squared, minus one, equals zero.

00:03:19.788 --> 00:03:21.881
So lambda is three, or one.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.594
For three, the direction one, one: the matrix sends

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:26.854
it to three, three.

00:03:27.045 --> 00:03:30.343
For one, the direction one, minus one, which it

00:03:30.205 --> 00:03:31.082
leaves alone.

00:03:31.292 --> 00:03:33.407
Here is why this matters for training.

00:03:33.622 --> 00:03:36.169
The bowl from lesson one, x squared plus three

00:03:36.031 --> 00:03:38.839
y squared, has a matrix of second derivatives:

00:03:38.974 --> 00:03:41.328
two, zero, zero, six.

00:03:41.542 --> 00:03:44.453
Its eigenvalues are the curvatures along the eigenvector

00:03:44.315 --> 00:03:47.373
directions: two along x, six along y.

00:03:47.587 --> 00:03:49.873
And the steepest curvature sets the learning-rate

00:03:49.735 --> 00:03:53.362
limit we found then: two divided by six, one third.

00:03:53.553 --> 00:03:56.305
The exact same number, from the eigenvalue.

00:03:57.807 --> 00:04:00.696
Every layer of a neural network is a matrix.

00:04:00.893 --> 00:04:04.225
The input vector goes in, W times x plus b comes

00:04:04.086 --> 00:04:06.838
out, and training is gradient descent on the entries

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:07.441
of W.

00:04:07.655 --> 00:04:10.895
In GPT-two, each of the 12 layers holds a matrix

00:04:10.757 --> 00:04:18.441
of 768 by 3072: 2,359,296 learned numbers in one

00:04:18.541 --> 00:04:21.134
matrix, and 124 million parameters in all.

00:04:21.292 --> 00:04:23.669
Attention is a matrix product too.

00:04:23.872 --> 00:04:26.976
Stack the query vectors as rows of Q, the key vectors

00:04:26.837 --> 00:04:29.976
as rows of K, and Q times K transpose computes

00:04:29.837 --> 00:04:32.544
every query-key dot product at once: a thousand

00:04:32.406 --> 00:04:34.908
tokens give a thousand by thousand grid of scores

00:04:34.769 --> 00:04:35.885
in one operation.

00:04:36.099 --> 00:04:38.942
That is why it runs so fast on a graphics processor,

00:04:39.065 --> 00:04:41.509
and why the 12 heads of each layer can run side

00:04:41.372 --> 00:04:42.146
by side.

00:04:42.333 --> 00:04:44.483
And the data itself is a matrix.

00:04:44.674 --> 00:04:45.620
A chest C.T.

00:04:45.481 --> 00:04:52.938
slice is 512 by 512 numbers, 262,144 pixel intensities.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.711
A convolution slides a small matrix across it,

00:04:55.822 --> 00:04:58.234
and the library rewrites that as one big matrix

00:04:58.094 --> 00:05:01.154
product, so the same hardware that trains GPT-two

00:05:01.015 --> 00:05:01.983
reads the scan.

00:05:03.432 --> 00:05:06.173
Google ranks web pages with an eigenvector.

00:05:06.382 --> 00:05:09.305
Build a matrix where each page passes its importance

00:05:09.166 --> 00:05:11.703
to the pages it links to, add a little damping,

00:05:11.814 --> 00:05:14.123
and the ranking is the vector that the matrix sends

00:05:13.984 --> 00:05:14.827
to itself.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.941
Apply the matrix again and again, and any starting

00:05:17.802 --> 00:05:19.168
guess settles onto it.

00:05:19.382 --> 00:05:22.816
Here, six pages: page three is linked by everyone,

00:05:22.893 --> 00:05:24.521
and the eigenvector says so.

00:05:25.208 --> 00:05:27.540
Recommendation is a matrix problem.

00:05:27.723 --> 00:05:31.714
Netflix's prize dataset had 100.48 million ratings

00:05:31.575 --> 00:05:37.896
from 480,189 users of 17,770 films: a table of

00:05:37.757 --> 00:05:41.597
8.5 billion cells with only 1.2 percent filled.

00:05:41.458 --> 00:05:43.847
The winning idea was to write the giant table as

00:05:43.708 --> 00:05:46.984
a product of two thin ones, users times hidden

00:05:47.026 --> 00:05:49.006
tastes, and tastes times films.

00:05:48.867 --> 00:05:52.017
Any matrix splits this way, into a sum of simple

00:05:52.106 --> 00:05:54.312
layers, each a column times a row, weighted by

00:05:54.174 --> 00:05:55.153
a singular value.

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:57.824
Keep the biggest few layers and you keep most of

00:05:57.685 --> 00:05:58.518
the structure.

00:05:58.727 --> 00:06:02.719
Here is a forty-eight by forty-eight image: 2,304

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:03.287
numbers.

00:06:03.477 --> 00:06:06.594
Six layers rebuild it with 582.

00:06:06.784 --> 00:06:09.730
The same trick, called LoRA, is how large language

00:06:09.591 --> 00:06:12.116
models are fine-tuned today: instead of changing

00:06:11.977 --> 00:06:18.821
a 768 by 768 weight matrix, 589,824 numbers, you

00:06:18.682 --> 00:06:23.642
learn two thin matrices of rank 8, 12,288 numbers,

00:06:23.765 --> 00:06:25.188
48 times fewer.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:27.245
So.

00:06:27.442 --> 00:06:30.331
A matrix moves space; its columns say where the

00:06:30.192 --> 00:06:31.228
basis lands.

00:06:31.431 --> 00:06:34.672
Multiplying matrices is composing moves, and order

00:06:34.533 --> 00:06:35.263
matters.

00:06:35.476 --> 00:06:37.649
The determinant is the area factor.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.502
Eigenvectors are the directions that only stretch,

00:06:40.624 --> 00:06:43.024
and their eigenvalues are the curvatures that limit

00:06:42.885 --> 00:06:44.183
how fast you can learn.

00:06:44.375 --> 00:06:45.491
Where in A.I.?

00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:48.582
Every layer is a matrix, attention is a matrix

00:06:48.443 --> 00:06:51.571
product, every image is a matrix, and fine-tuning

00:06:51.432 --> 00:06:52.843
is a low-rank matrix.

00:06:53.057 --> 00:06:56.252
How to use it: read a matrix by its columns, check

00:06:56.114 --> 00:06:58.480
the determinant before you invert, and let the

00:06:58.341 --> 00:07:00.638
largest eigenvalue set your learning rate.

00:07:00.841 --> 00:07:04.411
Next: softmax and cross-entropy, how a model turns

00:07:04.273 --> 00:07:05.934
scores into probabilities.
